ATTACK 3 — SURFACE ENUMERATION
  `grep -rn bucketMajorExpenses --include=*.go` (excluding _test.go) in
  the fixed tree: exactly two call sites, both in
  internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go (the chart builder line 1371
  and the drilldown line 358), both routed through the new
  splitPositiveMajorExpenseBuckets helper (lines 370 and 1372) — no
  third consumer, no competing sign filter. bucketMajorExpenses/
  majorExpenseBucket/splitPositiveMajorExpenseBuckets are all unexported
  to the dashboard package, so no other package can call them directly.
  internal/services/insights/trends.go's MajorExpenseTrends (a separate,
  independently-implemented Insights-page function) mentions
  bucketMajorExpenses only in a comment, computes its own per-name totals
  via a local sumByExpense closure, and applies NO sign filter at all —
  every name that appears in either period's totals map is included
  regardless of sign (confirmed by reading trends.go:19-110); this
  function already surfaces negative nets (fixed under CB3-D, not
  touched here) and is correctly out of this task's scope, not a second
  instance of the CB4 defect. internal/handlers/majorexpenses/verdict.go
  has an unrelated `total <= 0` guard (tracking-coverage percent, not a
  per-group visibility filter) — different feature, not a hit.
  web/static/js side: `grep -n Intl.NumberFormat charts.js` shows the new
  renderMajorExpenseCredits formatter (line 269) uses the identical
  'en-US' / style:'currency' / min-max 2 fraction digits configuration as
  the pre-existing renderMajorExpenseBreakdown formatter (line 207) — no
  dual-formatter divergence. `node -c charts.js` -> syntax OK.
  MET — no missed surface, no split classifier.

ATTACK 4 — UNMATCHED SIGN (spec-silence check)
  Confirmed unmatchedTotal CAN go negative on synthetic data: added a
  throwaway test (discarded, only in the throwaway copy) with a single
  declared-major-expense-free window containing one $50 outflow and one
  $300 refund, both unmatched -> unmatchedTotal = -250. Result: labels=[]
  (no "Unmatched" wedge, correctly suppressed by the pre-existing
  `if unmatchedTotal > 0` guard) AND result["credits"] key is ABSENT
  (creditBuckets was empty; there is no code path that ever adds
  "Unmatched" to credits — it is computed and used only inside
  buildMajorExpenseChartData, never routed through
  splitPositiveMajorExpenseBuckets). So a net-negative Unmatched total is
  computed, then silently vanishes from the chart response entirely: not
  a wedge, not in credits, not anywhere.
  This IS the same visibility gap CB4 fixes for declared major-expense
  groups, applied to the different "Unmatched" pseudo-group. However,
  reading CB4-RUN-SPEC.md closely: the title ("net-negative
  MAJOR-EXPENSE groups"), the motivating paragraph ("bucketMajorExpenses'
  total>0 filter hides any GROUP... while the Major Expenses page shows
  the SAME GROUP signed"), and all four numbered design-contract bullets
  are scoped to "every MATCHED group" / "the SHARED SOURCE" — i.e.
  bucketMajorExpenses' bucket list. Unmatched is a wholly separate code
  path (a local `unmatchedTotal` accumulator inside
  buildMajorExpenseChartData, pre-existing since before this task, with
  its own always-present `if unmatchedTotal > 0` guard) that the spec
  never names, references, or brings into the "SHARED SOURCE" contract.
  The spec is SILENT on Unmatched, not contradicted by this behavior.
  Per the adversarial-lane instruction ("FAIL if the spec's contract
  covers Unmatched, observation if the spec is silent"): this is an
  OBSERVATION for the backlog, not a FAIL. Recommend a follow-up task
  (same pattern as CB4: "Unmatched net-negative visibility") rather than
  reopening this one.

